Requirements: 29   with obligations: 28   deliberately none: 1

[task-01] For each way of failing that the review has recorded against a criterion, and
    each candidate test, the review records whether that test would fail if the
    delivered code contained that way of failing.
    -> test-failure-matrix-recording  [functional/explicit]
       For each way of failing recorded against a criterion and each candidate test,
       record whether that test would fail if the delivered code contained that way of
       failing.

[constraint-01] A pair is one recorded way of failing and one candidate test. Every pair
    is judged unless a path from that test to the source regions the way of failing
    implicates is proved absent. A pair whose path cannot be settled either way is
    judged.
    -> pair-judgement-unless-path-absent  [functional/explicit]
       A pair of one recorded way of failing and one candidate test is judged unless a
       path from that test to the source regions the way of failing implicates is proved
       absent.
    -> undecidable-path-pairs-are-judged  [functional/explicit]   (also serves completion-02)
       A pair whose path cannot be settled either way is judged.

[constraint-02] Every pair left unjudged is recorded, naming the way of failing, the
    test, and why it was not judged, and the review reports those records.
    -> record-unjudged-pairs  [functional/explicit]
       Every pair left unjudged is recorded, naming the way of failing, the test, and
       why it was not judged.
    -> report-unjudged-pair-records  [functional/explicit]
       The review reports the records for every pair left unjudged.

[constraint-03] The question put about a judged pair is whether that test would fail if
    the delivered code contained that way of failing.
    -> pair-question-matches-failure-test  [functional/explicit]
       For a judged pair, the question asks whether the candidate test would fail if the
       delivered code contained that way of failing.

[constraint-04] Pairs are judged in groups, and no group asks for more judgements in one
    request than a fixed limit allows.
    -> grouped-judgement-limit  [functional/explicit]
       Pairs are judged in groups, and each group requests no more judgements than the
       fixed limit allows.

[constraint-05] What comes back about a judged pair is which pair it is, its verdict,
    and a short reason, and nothing further.
    -> judged-pair-response-minimality  [functional/explicit]
       What comes back about a judged pair includes only the pair identity, its verdict,
       and a short reason.

[constraint-06] A verdict is reused rather than produced again exactly while both the
    way of failing it concerns and the source of the test it concerns are unchanged.
    -> verdict-reuse-when-way-and-test-source-unchanged  [regression/explicit]
       Reuse a verdict exactly when both the way of failing it concerns and the source
       of the test it concerns are unchanged.

[constraint-07] A verdict whose way of failing changed, or whose test's source changed,
    is produced again.
    -> reproduce-verdict-when-way-of-failing-or-test-source-changes  [regression/explicit]
       A verdict is produced again when the way of failing it concerns has changed or
       when the source of the test it concerns has changed.

[constraint-08] A run continuing an earlier run reuses every verdict it is entitled to
    reuse.
    -> reuse-entitled-verdicts-on-continuation  [functional/explicit]
       A continuing run reuses every verdict it is entitled to reuse.
    -> reproduce-only-nonreusable-verdicts-on-continuation  [functional/explicit]   (also serves constraint-09)
       A continuing run produces again only verdicts that are not entitled to be reused.

[constraint-09] A run continuing an earlier run produces again only the verdicts it is
    not entitled to reuse.
    -> reproduce-only-nonreusable-verdicts-on-continuation  [functional/explicit]   (also serves constraint-08)
       A continuing run produces again only verdicts that are not entitled to be reused.

[constraint-10] Where a verdict was reused rather than produced again, the review says
    so.
    -> reuse-says-so  [explanation_observability/explicit]
       Where a verdict is reused rather than produced again, the review says so.

[constraint-11] Whether the review reports the work complete, every rating it gives a
    criterion, and every test it recommends are what they would be if no pair had been
    judged at all.
    -> completion-report-complete-ratings-tests-as-if-no-pair-judged  [functional/explicit]   (also serves completion-07)
       The review reports the work complete, every rating it gives a criterion, and
       every test it recommends as they would be if no pair had been judged at all.

[constraint-12] The review reports, for each criterion, the support its judged pairs
    imply beside the rating the review gives that criterion today, and names the
    criteria where the two disagree.
    -> report-support-beside-rating  [functional/explicit]
       For each criterion, report the support implied by its judged pairs beside the
       rating the review gives that criterion today.
    -> name-disagreeing-criteria  [functional/explicit]
       Name the criteria where the support implied by judged pairs and the current
       rating disagree.

[exclusion-01] Producing the ways of failing that pairs are judged against.
    -> no-way-of-failing-production  [functional/explicit]
       The change does not produce the ways of failing that pairs are judged against.

[exclusion-02] Deriving a criterion's rating, the completion conclusion, or any
    recommended test from judged pairs.
    -> no-derive-rating-completion-or-recommendations-from-judged-pairs  [functional/explicit]
       The change does not derive a criterion's rating, the completion conclusion, or
       any recommended test from judged pairs.

[exclusion-03] Resolving a name to its definition, or following references beyond a
    single step, in order to decide whether a path is absent.
    -> no-name-resolution-or-deep-reference-following-for-path-absence  [compatibility/explicit]
       The change does not resolve a name to its definition or follow references beyond
       a single step in order to decide whether a path is absent.

[exclusion-04] Which criteria the review derives from the mandate, and how it derives
    them.
    -> do-not-derive-criteria-from-mandate  [functional/explicit]
       The change does not derive which criteria the review derives from the mandate, or
       how it derives them.

[exclusion-05] How the review gathers, maps, judges or rates test evidence today, and
    retiring any part of it.
    -> no-change-to-test-evidence-workflow  [functional/explicit]
       The change does not alter how the review gathers, maps, judges, or rates test
       evidence today, and it does not retire any part of that workflow.

[exclusion-06] Choosing between candidate shapes for the request that carries the pair
    question.
    -> request-shape-choice-excluded  [functional/explicit]
       The change does not include choosing between candidate shapes for the request
       that carries the pair question.

[exclusion-07] Running the delivered code or any test.
    -> no-runtime-or-test-execution  [functional/explicit]
       The change does not run the delivered code or any test.

[completion-01] Implementation.
    -- no obligation, deliberately
       The requirement is a section marker standing alone: "Implementation."

[completion-02] A test fails when a pair whose path cannot be settled either way is left
    unjudged rather than judged.
    -> undecidable-path-pairs-are-judged  [functional/explicit]   (also serves constraint-01)
       A pair whose path cannot be settled either way is judged.
    -> test-covers-unsettled-path-pair-judgment  [test_demand/explicit]
       A test asserts that a pair whose path cannot be settled either way is judged
       rather than left unjudged.

[completion-03] A test fails when a pair is left unjudged without a record naming the
    way of failing, the test and the reason.
    -> record-unjudged-pair-reason  [test_demand/explicit]
       A test fails when a pair is left unjudged without a record naming the way of
       failing, the test, and the reason.

[completion-04] A test asserts that adding one candidate test between two continued runs
    produces new verdicts only for pairs concerning that test.
    -> continued-run-new-verdicts-test  [test_demand/explicit]
       A test asserts that adding one candidate test between two continued runs produces
       new verdicts only for pairs concerning that test.

[completion-05] A test fails when a verdict is produced again across two continued runs
    although its way of failing and its test's source are both unchanged.
    -> verdict-not-reproduced-when-unchanged  [test_demand/explicit]
       A test fails when a verdict is produced again across two continued runs although
       its way of failing and its test's source are both unchanged.

[completion-06] A test fails when a verdict is reused although its test's source
    changed.
    -> reuse-verdict-only-when-test-source-unchanged  [functional/explicit]
       A verdict is reused only when the test's source is unchanged.

[completion-07] A test fails when judging pairs changes the review's completion
    conclusion, any criterion's rating, or which tests it recommends.
    -> completion-report-complete-ratings-tests-as-if-no-pair-judged  [functional/explicit]   (also serves constraint-11)
       The review reports the work complete, every rating it gives a criterion, and
       every test it recommends as they would be if no pair had been judged at all.

[completion-08] A test asserts the report shows, for each criterion, the support its
    judged pairs imply beside the rating the review gives that criterion today.
    -> report-support-beside-rating-test  [test_demand/explicit]
       A test asserts that the report shows, for each criterion, the support its judged
       pairs imply beside the rating the review gives that criterion today.

[completion-09] Two recorded runs over the same input produce byte-identical review
    state and byte-identical report output.
    -> byte-identical-review-state-and-report-output  [regression/explicit]
       Two recorded runs over the same input produce byte-identical review state and
       byte-identical report output.
